  "articleBody": "When you're installing motion sensor lighting, it's easy to make a few common blunders that can throw off its performance. To make sure your setup works flawlessly, here are some mistakes to look out for:  1. **Poor Sensor Placement:** If your sensor isn't placed where traffic crosses its field of view, it won't trigger properly. Make sure it has a clear line of sight to the area you want to cover.  2. **Wrong Outdoor Rating:** Using an indoor-rated fixture outside is a recipe for trouble. Always choose fixtures appropriately rated for outdoor exposure, especially wet-rated ones where rain or snow is a factor.  3. **Incompatible Dimmers or Bulbs:** Not all motion sensors play well with every dimmer or bulb type. Double-check compatibility to avoid flickering or unreliable activation.  4. **Incorrect Sensitivity Settings:** Setting the sensitivity too high can lead to nuisance switching from pets or passing cars, while too low means missed triggers. Adjust it carefully for optimal performance.  5. **Ignoring Response Time:** Think about how quickly you need the light to activate and how long it should stay on after motion stops. Adjust these settings to match your needs for convenience and energy saving.  If you hit a snag with hard-wiring, load calculations, or persistent issues, calling in a pro can prevent headaches and ensure your motion sensor lighting is a perfect addition to your home.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
